A flow of rich text, rendered by html.

The span family is an ordinary component tree and this is a platform
implementing it: `codegen/platform/html/html.sngl` names the element html
already has for what each member means, so `bold` is `<strong>` and a link is
`<a>`, and the nesting an author wrote carries straight over. The cascade is
CSS's own -- the five typography fields a `SpanStyle` carries are exactly the
ones a browser inherits -- so nothing flattens the tree and no pass knows what
a span is.

Four claims are in the output below and each is here because it could go
wrong somewhere else:

  - **Escaping.** `<`, `&` and a quote in an author's words come out as
    entities, which is the reason a flow is rendered by a platform rather than
    by a library writing markup into `innerHTML`.
  - **Literal whitespace.** Two spaces are two spaces and a newline ends the
    line. html is the one host that would otherwise collapse them, so the flow
    carries `white-space: pre-wrap` and the static renderer stops indenting
    inside it -- the pretty-printer's own newlines would be visible text.
  - **Both nesting orders are one answer.** `monospace { link { … } }` and
    `link { monospace { … } }` are `<code><a>` and `<a><code>`: different
    trees, same words in the same face leading to the same place.
  - **A role is a semantic.** A heading announces as one, a quotation is
    quoted, a code sample is preformatted. That is what `role` is *for*, as
    against `style`, which is what the block components set beside it.

`image` is the one member whose description is a string rather than spans: no
formatting is available in one, every host that has a description has a plain
attribute, and the quote and ampersand in this fixture's `alt` are there to
show the attribute is escaped like any other.

`token` emits a class and never a color: what a keyword looks like is the
page's to say, and a document naming one would be unreadable against a theme
it never heard of.

bubbletea is the third target and the first host with no inheritance of its
own: lipgloss renders a string and hands back a string, so the cascade an
author wrote is flattened in the emitter and each run of words is rendered
exactly once, with the style it ends up with. Rendering a joined flow a second
time is not the tidier alternative it looks like -- the runs inside it have
left their reset sequences in the string, and an outer color would stop at the
first of them -- which is why a flow's own typography seeds the words rather
than styling the block, and why the block style below carries padding and a
background and no weight.

Three things a terminal answers differently and each is visible above. It has
one face and one size, so `monospace` renders as the words it was already
going to render and a heading is bold rather than large. It cannot follow a
link, so the URL is dropped and the words are underlined, which is the trade
`ui.link` already makes. And a `token` is one of the terminal's own sixteen
colors rather than a value this document chose -- the same argument the html
class makes, answered with the palette the reader's terminal already has.

Both html modes are here and both write the same markup. Route mode renders
the page server-side through a second emitter, so it is the one that could
disagree -- and it did: a run's style is a set of CSS declarations rather than
an attribute value, and a prop neither literal nor state-dependent is refused
outright there, so the build failed until both renderers asked one function for
an element's inline CSS.

deny * `innerHTML` -- text reaches the page as text, never as markup
deny * `<richText` -- the family is implemented, not emitted under its own name
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `Bold\(true\)\.Render\(content_` -- a flow's weight seeds its words; applied to the joined string, the first inner reset would end it partway along the line
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `JoinHorizontal` -- a flow concatenates its words, where joining them would put each run in a column of its own
-- doc.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import md "sngl:ui/markup"

output {
    go {
        html
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

ui.window #home(title="doc") {
    ui.vbox {
        md.heading1 {
            md.text(value="Title")
        }
        md.paragraph {
            md.text(value="a < b & \"c\"  two spaces\nand a newline")
        }
        md.paragraph {
            md.monospace {
                md.link(href="https://example.com/?a=1&b=2") {
                    md.text(value="mono link")
                }
            }
            md.link(href="https://example.com/") {
                md.monospace {
                    md.text(value="link mono")
                }
            }
        }
        md.codeBlock {
            md.token(kind=md.Token.keyword) {
                md.text(value="func")
            }
            md.text(value=" ")
            md.token(kind=md.Token.function) {
                md.text(value="main")
            }
        }
        md.paragraph {
            md.underline {
                md.strike {
                    md.italic {
                        md.text(value="all three")
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        md.paragraph {
            md.text(value="before ")
            md.image(src="/icon.png", alt="an \"icon\" & more")
            md.text(value=" after")
        }
        md.paragraph {
            md.run(style={color=#336699, fontSize=20px, fontWeight=md.Weight.bold}) {
                md.text(value="a styled run")
            }
        }
        md.quote {
            md.text(value="quoted")
        }
        md.caption {
            md.text(value="a caption")
        }
    }
}
